SHELLRAM                                                       [3.0]
--------

Format:      SHELLRAM [ON | OFF [/T]]

Purpose:     Displays or sets the use of a RAM segment for  extended
        shell features.

Use:         COMMAND3.COM normally allocates one 16K segment of  the
        mapped RAM and keeps the command history,  the alias list
        and its own state during the execution of transient programs
        in it.

             If no parameters are given,  then the current state  is
        displayed.

             SHELLRAM OFF gives the segment back to the system,  for
        users who need every RAM segment they can get for some other
        program.  The command history,  the aliases and the  %_SHELL%
        variable stop working (ALIAS, HISTORY  and  MEMORY  report
        'Shell RAM is off'),  and the TPA shrinks by  about  1K,
        which COMMAND3.COM uses to save its state below its resident
        code, as versions older than 2.40 did.

             SHELLRAM ON returns to the normal state,  allocating  a
        RAM segment again.  The command history and the  alias  list
        come back empty,  unless a segment left over from a previous
        shell could be adopted.  'Not enough memory' is reported and
        nothing changes when no free segment exists.

             The state is recorded in the SHELLRAM environment item,
        which  is  read  when  the  command  interpreter  starts: it
        survives entering the BASIC interpreter  and  coming    back
        with CALL SYSTEM.  Setting the item directly with SET   also
        works,  taking effect the next  time the interpreter is
        loaded (see HELP ENV on Environment Items).

             With the /T (temporary) option the state is changed but
        not recorded in the environment item,  so the next start  of
        the command interpreter goes back to the recorded state.

             The state can not be changed from a shell  that  shares
        its segment with an outer shell (see HELP COMMAND3 on invok-
        ing a second command interpreter).

             When no free RAM segment exists at the time the command
        interpreter starts,  it starts with the shell RAM off, as if
        SHELLRAM OFF had been given.  This automatic fallback is not
        recorded in the environment item,  so the normal state    is
        attempted again on the next start.

Examples:

        SHELLRAM
        Shell RAM is ON

             No parameters were given,  so the current state is dis-
        played, in this case the normal one.

        SHELLRAM OFF

             The RAM segment is given back to the system.

        SHELLRAM OFF /T

             The RAM segment is given back to the system,  but  the
        next start of the command interpreter will allocate one again.
